## How to give credit

If you reuse material from this repository, the attribution does not need to be complicated. A clear credit with the author, repository, link, and license is usually enough.

A simple example for a website, presentation, figure caption, or report is:

> Adapted from Roman Zakoldaev, *Laser microplasma notes*, GitHub, https://github.com/zakoldaev/laser-microplasma-notes, licensed under CC BY 4.0.

If you copied material without changing it, use **“Source:”** instead of **“Adapted from:”**.

For an academic reference, a fuller citation can be written as:

> Zakoldaev, R. (2026). *Laser microplasma notes*. GitHub repository. https://github.com/zakoldaev/laser-microplasma-notes

If you use a specific note, it is even better to name that note and link directly to it, for example:

> Roman Zakoldaev, “From beam correction to a Raman micro-probe,” in *Laser microplasma notes*, GitHub, 2026, CC BY 4.0.

If you modify a figure, diagram, text passage, or simulation, please make that clear, for example:

> Adapted from Roman Zakoldaev, *Laser microplasma notes* (CC BY 4.0); modified by the present author.

For academic software and data workflows, the repository also contains `CITATION.cff`, which GitHub can use to generate citation information through the **Cite this repository** option when available.

